`attestation_mr_changed()` bumps `att->log->generation`, but the entry's `mr_generation` was already computed as `log->generation` + 1 in `attestation_mr_extend_locked()` and the slot reserved at that index. This couples two places by convention (compute N+1, call `changed()` which makes it N+1, append at N+1). If any future caller invokes `attestation_mr_changed()` without appending (or the reverse) the generation/index invariant silently breaks. Have one function that takes the next generation, stores the entry, and publishes it. Also, `log_init` uses `++log->generation` directly while extend uses `mr_changed()`: three different styles for the same counter.

Also, `att->mr_read_generation` is incremented in lockstep with `log->generation` and is never otherwise modified; at this point it is a duplicate counter of `log->generation` (initial values: `log->generation` starts at `mrs_count` after `log_init`, `mr_read_generation` starts at 0, so they are not even equal, which looks accidental). Is a second counter needed? If it must differ (read-cache invalidation vs. journal), document why; otherwise use one. (Its only reader is `ctlv.c`, two patches later.)

