Duplicate validation: this checks `values->values[i].algorithm` == `props->algorithms[i]`, which (given the ordered, validated props) already implies everything that `attestation_digest_set_copy()` re-checks (range, non-zero digest size, strictly increasing). Pick one. Also nothing checks the digest 'data' length: `struct attestation_digest` has a fixed `HASH_MAX_DIGESTSIZE` array while the true length is `hash_digest_size[algorithm]`; the bytes beyond are copied verbatim into the long-lived journal (`kmemdup_array`) and, if user-supplied via `mr_extend`, can hold anything. They are not leaked on encode (encode uses `digest_size`), but copying 64*N bytes per entry into an unbounded journal is wasteful; store compact digests.

> +				   const struct attestation_digest_set *values);
> +int attestation_mr_log_init(struct attestation *att);

This function is far more complicated than needed. It counts MRs, allocates a temporary pointer array, allocates every INFO entry, then attaches `att->log`, then inserts, with two overlapping error labels (`err_attached` falls through into `err_infos` then `err_log`, with 'log = `NULL`' tricks to avoid a double free). I traced the paths and did not find a double free, but it is fragile. A single loop doing 'alloc info; `xa_insert`; on error call `attestation_mr_log_free(log)`' needs no temp array at all (entries already inserted are owned by the log; the one in flight is freed locally). The temp array only exists to separate allocation from generation numbering, which has no purpose.

'if (`log->generation` == `UINT_MAX`) { ret = `-EOVERFLOW`; ...}' inside the init loop is unreachable in practice (it needs 2^32 MRs) - dead code. The per-MR iteration order is xarray (id) order, so generations of INFO entries follow MR id order; fine but undocumented.

'int ret = `-ENOMEM`;' initial value is used only for the kcalloc failure path - set it explicitly there. 'count && !infos' - when count == 0 kcalloc returns `ZERO_SIZE_PTR` so the && is only needed for that case; simply skip the allocation if count == 0.

> +struct attestation_mr_log_entry_info *attestation_mr_info_alloc(const struct attestation_mr *mr);

Why deep-copy name and algorithms? The documentation in 0015 states "Their property storage remains valid until `ctlv_destroy()` or failed-creation unwind completes", and the journal is freed in `core_fini()` which runs strictly before that point. The INFO entry could reference `mr->props` directly; the kstrdup/`kmemdup_array`, three-label unwind and the matching `kfree()`s in `attestation_mr_log_entry_free()` are unnecessary. If the copy is deliberate (e.g. provider-module unload while journal readers exist), that contradicts the lifetime contract and should be stated.

Also the function is non-static (declared in `internal.h`) but only used in `mr.c`. And error returns are `ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM)` hard-coded, ok.

