Summary:
Most serious issues:
- Unbounded, un-accounted, append-only kernel memory journal (one heap entry per extend, each carrying full `HASH_MAX_DIGESTSIZE`-sized digests); at `UINT_MAX` the whole instance is silently stopped via `attestation_stop()`. Needs a cap / memcg accounting and a log message.
- Documentation claims "infallible publication" and "allocates the complete journal entry and event before invoking `mr_extend`", but event emission/encoding can fail (only `pr_err`) and the xarray slot is reserved after prepare, inside `extend_locked`. Event size vs MR digest-set size is never validated up front, so the failure surfaces only after the hardware was extended.
- On `mr_extend()` failure no generation/`mr_read_generation` update, though the hardware might have changed; `mr_read_generation` duplicates `log->generation` and can wrap.
- Needless complexity and dead code: INFO entries deep-copy immutable props; `mr_log_init` uses a temp array and a `UINT_MAX` check that is unreachable; one-member `event_batch`; size/type params of `extend_alloc` with one caller; `struct attestation_mr` wrapper; separately allocated log; heap-allocated `mr_change`; duplicated props/info encoders; duplicated validation; `xa_erase` before `xa_destroy`.
- `attestation_mr_log_append` ignores `xa_store()` result relying on a non-local reservation.
- Whole patch has no caller until 0017 (unbuildable, unreviewable in isolation); journal allocation in `post_init()` adds a needless failure mode.
